# The robot deploy ships the tree via `git archive | ssh tar -x`, often from a Windows
# checkout. Anything executed or parsed on the CM4 must stay LF: CRLF breaks systemd
# units and shebangs on the robot (a CRLF shebang makes systemd fail with 203/EXEC
# "No such file or directory").
#
# THE DEFAULT BELOW IS LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness. `git archive` honours these
# attributes, but for any path NOT listed here it falls back to core.autocrlf --
# which is `true` on Windows checkouts. So an enumerated list silently ships
# CRLF for every extension someone forgets to add.
# `text=auto` still leaves genuine binaries (.png, .onnx) untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Kept explicit: these are the ones that break loudly if the default is ever
# weakened, and they document WHY LF matters on the CM4.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.conf    text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.jsonl   text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf

# Windows-side scripts stay native. A batch file with LF endings misparses labels and
# blocks in cmd.exe, so Deploy-to-Robot.cmd must reach Windows as CRLF.
*.ps1     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.bat     text eol=crlf
